      <title>Zimbabwean author Simbarashe Kundizeza denies using AI to write prize-winning story</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/oct/14/zimbabwean-author-simbarashe-kundizeza-denies-ai-story</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Article in Atlantic claims The Blue Fighter was artificially generated, days after it won Caine prize for African writers&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Zimbabwean writer Simbarashe Kundizeza has denied using AI to write a prize-winning short story after an article in the Atlantic said software had flagged it as almost completely AI-generated.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kundizeza’s story &lt;a href="https://static1.squarespace.com/static/6a4f3fb6a6178e0225b39a4c/t/6a95fffc36f1fc568e02f7fe/1788215293124/3.The+Blue+Fighter.pdf"&gt;The Blue Fighter&lt;/a&gt; was announced as the winner of the prestigious Caine prize for African writers on 6 October. Five days later, The Atlantic published an &lt;a href="https://www.theatlantic.com/books/2026/10/caine-prize-winning-short-story-seems-be-ai/688967/"&gt;article&lt;/a&gt; by the author and journalist Vauhini Vara titled: “Yet Another Literary prize winner Seems to Be AI.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/oct/14/zimbabwean-author-simbarashe-kundizeza-denies-ai-story"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
