    <item>
      <title>Latin American organised crime ‘may operate in 16 European countries’</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/oct/13/latin-america-organised-crime-cartels-drugs-cocaine-europe</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Cocaine seizures in Europe are up more than fivefold in 10 years as a host of cartels take root. A four-month Guardian investigation reveals the true scale of a continent-wide shift&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A four-month investigation by the Guardian has revealed that the surge in cocaine trafficking to Europe is directly linked to the expanding presence of Latin American criminal networks in Europe, where they have forged close ties with local mafia organisations.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The investigation, utilising databases, police and intelligence agencies, along with interviews with judges, prosecutors and experts from eight countries, shows that cartels and factions from Colombia, Brazil, Ecuador, Venezuela, Mexico and the US are active in at least 12 European countries. Between 2014 and 2024, there was a &lt;a href="https://www.euda.europa.eu/publications/european-drug-report/2026/drug-supply-production-and-precursors_en"&gt;471% rise in the quantity of cocaine seized&lt;/a&gt; and more local laboratories processing cocaine derivatives were dismantled.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/oct/13/latin-america-organised-crime-cartels-drugs-cocaine-europe"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
